### 1. tegister a new user - should return both user info AND a token
POST http://localhost:8080/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "testuser3",
  "email": "testuser3@example.com",
  "password": "somepassword123"
}


### 2. try a protected route with NO token at all - should 401
POST http://localhost:8080/users/watchlist
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "movieId": 1
}


### 3. try a protected route with a valid token - should succeed
### Replace <PASTE_TOKEN_HERE> with the real token from step 1's response
POST http://localhost:8080/users/watchlist
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJ1c2VySWQiOjYsImV4cCI6MTc4NTcyOTAzMH0.VcnLqKb7OjnMmD3kFs23H-hkvjdoblbbYscs46QPT1o

{
  "movieId": 1
}


### 4. try with a garbage/invalid token - should 401
POST http://localhost:8080/users/watchlist
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer this.is.not.a.real.token

{
  "movieId": 1
}


### 5. login with the same user - should also return a working token
POST http://localhost:8080/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "testuser3@example.com",
  "password": "somepassword123"
}


### 6. confirm GET routes still work with NO token (should be public)
GET http://localhost:8080/movies